---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import Gloss from '../../components/Gloss.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import DataTable from '../../components/DataTable.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const press = assertion('ninety-nine-papers-and-two-of-them-online')

const pressSpec = bar(
  press.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  press.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Newspaper titles printed, 1843–2026', labelValues: true },
)

const limaNewsLineage = [
  ['The Daily Democratic Times', '27 Oct 1884', 'v.1 no.1'],
  ['The Lima Daily Democratic Times', 'to 2 Feb 1889', '–v.5 no.89'],
  ['The Lima Daily Times', '4 Feb 1889', 'v.5 no.90 – v.9 no.141'],
  ['The Lima Times-Democrat', '1 Apr 1893', 'v.9 no.142 – v.11 no.287'],
  ['The Times-Democrat', '18 Sep 1895', 'v.11 no.288 – ceased 1901'],
  ['The Lima Times-Democrat', '9 Apr 1901', 'v.17 no.151 – 36th yr no.122'],
  ['The Lima Daily News and Times-Democrat', '3 May 1920', '36th year'],
  ['The Lima News and Times-Democrat', '6 Jun 1920', '36th – 43rd year'],
  ['The Lima News', '21 Jun 1926', '43rd year – current'],
]
---

<Article slug="ninety-nine-papers">
  <AssertionCard assertion={press} />
  <p>
      Ninety-nine newspapers have been printed in this county, sixty-six of them in Lima, and two
      of the ninety-nine are digitized. One is the Bluffton weekly, the only Allen County title the
      Library of Congress holds. The other is a Lima paper — 810 issues of
      <em>The Times-Democrat</em>, 2 November 1900 to 1 March 1912, on Ohio Memory. It is not the
      daily of that name: 405 of the 810 fall on a Friday and 404 on a Tuesday, and 1903, 1904 and
      1908 are missing altogether.
    </p>
    <Gloss>
      For six phases the Bluffton weekly was the only paper read here, and the absence of a Lima
      paper was reported each time — twice without naming the archive it was absent from. The
      second archive was never searched until it was.
    </Gloss>
  <ChartFigure
    id="county-papers"
    title="Allen County newspaper titles, 1843–2026, by place of publication"
    caption="Ninety-seven of the ninety-nine records name a place of publication inside the county; the other two were printed at Springfield and at Wapakoneta and filed under Allen. Dates are the cataloguer's earliest and latest sighted issue, and forty-five of the ninety-nine leave one end open."
    columns={['Town', 'Titles']}
    rows={press.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="county-papers" set:html={JSON.stringify(pressSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Forty-three of the ninety-nine have no library reporting a copy anywhere. Of the fifty-six
    that do, the Ohio History Connection holds runs of forty-three and the Lima Public Library of
    nineteen. That is a count of what was reported to a union list, not a proof that nothing
    survives — but it is what a reader with a research library card can and cannot reach.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first was <em>The Porcupine</em>, a Democratic weekly whose volume 1, number 1 is dated
    4 March 1843 — eleven months after Lima had a town government at all. It lasted seven months.
    Eleven issues of it survive, all on one reel in Columbus.
  </p>
  <p>
    The oldest thing still running is a volume count. The Lima News has carried one unbroken issue
    number through nine titles since 27 October 1884, each successor opening at the number after
    its predecessor's last:
  </p>
  <DataTable
    title="One issue count, nine mastheads"
    caption="In the merger of May 1920 the paper kept the News's name and the Times-Democrat's age: the Lima Daily News ended its 24th year on 2 May and the combined paper appeared the next day as the 36th."
    columns={['Title', 'From', 'Numbering']}
    rows={limaNewsLineage}
  />
  <p>
    And a strike is legible from three catalogue records and nothing else. The Lima News was
    suspended from 10 May to 27 June 1957. <em>The Bulletin</em> — numbers 1 to 4, on exactly
    those dates, imprint "Lima, Ohio : Union Newspaper Workers" — was published, in the
    cataloguer's words, by "striking and locked out workers of the Lima news". Four days after the
    News came back, Lima had a second daily: the Lima Citizen, 1 July 1957 to 7 January 1964. It
    is the last competitor the city has had.
  </p>
</Article>
